From 1ee4ffe85d18337b5412b094c973639b93fd01ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kaden <kaden@localhost>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 12:38:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/16] cuda: stop dividing in the elementwise and norm kernels
 on Pascal

sm_60 has no integer divider, so the 64-bit divisions several small
kernels did per element cost dozens of instructions each and dominated
them. Four changes, all general:

- binbcast: a flat fast path for when every operand has the
  destination's shape and is contiguous, skipping the broadcast index
  math entirely (3.07 -> 2.09 us)
- cpy_scalar: eight 64-bit divisions per element replaced with the
  existing 32-bit multiply-shift helpers (4.85 -> 2.54 us)
- the gated unary kernels and concat_cont: same, two per element
- the norm kernels: load the row four floats at a time. A single block
  owns the row, so what limits it is the SM's requests in flight rather
  than bandwidth, and one float4 request carries four times the payload

29.32 -> ~29.9 t/s on 2x Tesla P100 with Qwen3 27B Q6_K.

Perplexity 2.7565 +/- 0.02153 against 2.7554 +/- 0.02151 for stock: the
norm reductions now sum in a different order, so the result is no longer
bit-identical, but the shift is 5% of the error bar. The index-math
changes are bit-exact. test-backend-ops passes RMS_NORM 51/51,
RMS_NORM_MUL_ADD 30/30, L2_NORM 20/20, NORM 50/50, ADD 99/99, MUL 91/91,
CPY 246/246, CONCAT 177/177, SWIGLU 24/24, SET_ROWS 159/159.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AaVsfEiPALnEHpNH9UZbjk
